concern:
  heading: "Brand voice, and not writing yourself into a compliance problem"
  body: >
    <p>Two things agencies raise. The first is voice — generic AI copy is obvious, and
    an agency's writing is part of its brand. The program spends real time on making the
    output sound like your agency specifically, using your existing listings as the
    reference rather than accepting the default register.</p>
    <p>The second matters more. Generated property copy will happily invent a feature,
    round a number the wrong way, or produce a price statement that creates an
    underquoting or misleading-conduct problem. Australian consumer law does not care
    that a tool wrote it. We train the review step as a non-negotiable part of the
    workflow, with particular attention to price, measurements, zoning and anything
    described as a fact about the property.</p>
faq:
  - q: "Can this be rolled out across a franchise network?"
    a: "Yes, and it's the most efficient way to buy it. The program runs the same for one office or twenty, and a network gets a shared standard for voice, review and compliance rather than each office improvising. Talk to us about multi-office pricing."
  - q: "Will it replace our copywriter?"
    a: "It changes what they spend the day on. Agencies that keep a copywriter tend to move them up the chain — campaign strategy, the flagship listings, and owning the review standard — rather than producing forty listings a month by hand."
  - q: "Do agents actually use it, or does it die after the training?"
    a: "That's the right question, and it's why the program runs over three months rather than as a one-day course. People build the workflows on their own live listings during the program, so by the end it's already how they work. Ongoing support keeps it current after that."
  - q: "What about property managers, not just sales?"
    a: "Property management usually sees the faster return. The correspondence volume is higher, more of it is routine, and the emotional cost of writing arrears and maintenance messages all day is real. We cover both sides."
  - q: "Is our vendor and buyer data safe?"
    a: "It is if the tools are set up properly, and that setup is part of the training — which tools have business terms, what should never be pasted in, and how to keep client details out of anything that trains on your inputs."
